  <h2>How local PDF chat works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Extract:</strong> the PDF's text is pulled out and split into sections and passages.</li>
    <li><strong>Index:</strong> each passage becomes a vector with an <a href={href('glossary/embeddings/')}>embedding</a> model, stored in a local <a href={href('glossary/vector-database/')}>vector database</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Retrieve:</strong> your question is matched against the passages; the closest ones are selected.</li>
    <li><strong>Answer:</strong> only those passages go to the model, which answers and cites them. This is <a href={href('glossary/rag/')}>retrieval-augmented generation</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Short papers can be sent to the model whole, which avoids missing context. Long ones need the retrieval step.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Install <a href="https://ollama.com/">Ollama</a> to run a <a href={href('glossary/local-llm/')}>local LLM</a>, and pull a model, for example <code>ollama pull qwen3:8b</code>.</li>
    <li>Pick a reader that works with it and stores your library locally.</li>
    <li>Try a question you know the answer to, and check that the cited passage really says it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to look for in a tool</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Where does the PDF text go?</td><td>Local storage keeps unpublished work private</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Can it use a local model?</td><td>No text leaves your machine, and it costs nothing per question</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Do answers cite the page?</td><td>You can check claims instead of trusting them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Are AI notes marked?</td><td>You can tell your thinking from the model's later</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
